From 71e5b116e3316be877653368ac8a4b015753ebdd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 18:20:38 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: play music mono instead of stereo

Last remaining item from the dossage-24 perf review, tried per explicit
user direction after the rest of the backlog closed out with no
measurable change from -fomit-frame-pointer. The audio-pump loop
(patches 0007/0008) is a real, if secondary, per-frame cost (~20-25ms
in the last real-hardware measurement) -- going mono halves the bytes
the pump loop has to push per chunk for the same real-time duration.

This is a genuine fidelity tradeoff, not a free win: Passage's music
has real, intentionally-authored independent per-note left/right
loudness (mLoudnessLeft/mLoudnessRight, decoded from music.tga's
separate green/red channels -- verified by reading the source, not
assumed) -- collapsing to mono discards that. Flagged explicitly to the
user before implementing; they chose to try it and get a real data
point rather than leave it unmeasured.

The actual synthesis pipeline (synthesizeAudioCallback, Timbre,
Envelope) is completely untouched -- the offline render tool
(tools/render-music/render_music.cpp, an outer-repo file, changed
separately) still renders full-fidelity stereo through the exact same
code path as before, then downmixes to mono (average L+R, clamped by
construction since both inputs are already in valid Sint16 range) only
at the very last step, when writing the WAV file. No risk to the
musical content itself, only to the final shipped format.

Two changes needed here to match the new mono asset, both required for
correctness (a channel-count mismatch between the WAV and the audio
stream's declared format doesn't just lose fidelity -- SDL would
misinterpret consecutive mono samples as alternating stereo channels,
audible as roughly half-speed wrong-pitch playback, not just quieter):

1. `startMusic()`: `audioFormat.channels` 2 -> 1, matching the
   downmixed SONG.WAV.
2. `audioCallback()`: the "round up to a whole number of frames"
   convention changes from 4 bytes/frame (stereo S16) to 2 bytes/frame
   (mono S16) -- the only place this function assumed stereo
   specifically; everything else (songReadPos, numSongSamples,
   wraparound) was already sample-count-based, not frame-pair-based,
   so it needed no changes to work correctly with mono.

Also regenerated `music/SONG.WAV` itself via the updated render tool --
2,998,844 bytes (down from ~5.7MB stereo), confirmed exactly half the
prior PCM data size as expected.

Verified under DOSBox-X: builds; SONG.WAV loads without error (no
"Unable to load"/"Unable to open audio" messages); boots, plays, exits
cleanly via ESCAPE, no hang; PUMPDBG.LOG shows the pump loop's own
iteration count dropping (several 20-frame windows read avg_iters=0,
meaning the ring already had enough depth without pumping at all --
consistent with mono chunks representing more real-time audio per byte
pumped, the same mechanism the earlier 11025Hz fix relied on).
Cannot verify actual audible correctness (pitch/tempo/tone) from this
sandboxed environment -- no real audio capture available locally.
Real-hardware confirmation (fps, PUMPDBG.LOG, and specifically whether
the music sounds normal-speed/normal-pitch, not the half-speed symptom
a channel-count bug would produce) is essential, not optional, for
this patch.
---
 gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp |  24 ++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp b/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp
index dd39cbd..1efd301 100644
--- a/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/musicPlayer.cpp
@@ -370,9 +370,11 @@ void audioCallback( void *inUserData, SDL_AudioStream *inStream,
         return;
         }
 
-    // round up to a whole number of stereo S16 frames (4 bytes/frame),
-    // same convention synthesizeAudioCallback above uses
-    int numBytes = ( ( inAdditionalAmount + 3 ) / 4 ) * 4;
+    // round up to a whole number of mono S16 frames (2 bytes/frame) --
+    // SONG.WAV is mono now (see startMusic's own comment on
+    // audioFormat.channels), NOT the stereo 4-bytes/frame convention
+    // synthesizeAudioCallback above (and this function, before) used.
+    int numBytes = ( ( inAdditionalAmount + 1 ) / 2 ) * 2;
 
     Uint8 *outputBuffer = new Uint8[ numBytes ];
 
@@ -700,11 +702,21 @@ void startMusic( const char *inTGAFileName ) {
 
     SDL_AudioSpec audioFormat;
 
-    /* Set 16-bit stereo audio at sampleRate (11025Hz -- see the comment
-       above the global) */
+    /* Set 16-bit MONO audio at sampleRate (11025Hz -- see the comment
+       above the global). DOS-PORT: SONG.WAV is rendered mono (see
+       tools/render-music/render_music.cpp's downmix step) specifically
+       to halve the audio-pump loop's per-chunk byte count -- real-
+       hardware measurement found that loop a real, if secondary,
+       per-frame cost. This is a deliberate fidelity tradeoff (Passage's
+       music has genuine independent per-note left/right loudness,
+       collapsed here), not a bug -- must match audioCallback's own
+       mono-frame-size assumption below and the WAV file's actual
+       channel count exactly, or SDL will misinterpret consecutive mono
+       samples as alternating stereo channels (audible as roughly
+       half-speed, wrong-pitch playback, not just quieter). */
     audioFormat.freq = sampleRate;
     audioFormat.format = SDL_AUDIO_S16;
-    audioFormat.channels = 2;
+    audioFormat.channels = 1;
 
 #ifdef __DJGPP__
     // DOS-PORT: request the PHYSICAL device open at sampleRate too, not
